s,\r\n        *,\r\n        on_change: Optional[ChangeCallback] = None,\r\n        owner_thread: Optional[int] = None,\r\n        validate_mesh: Optional[Callable[[MeshArrays], None]] = None,\r\n        scalar_range: Optional[tuple[float,float]] = None,\r\n        colormap: object = None,\r\n    ) -> None:\r\n        if not isinstance(mesh, MeshArrays):\r\n            raise TypeError(\"mesh must be MeshArrays\")\r\n", "numLines": 50, "startLine": 16, "requestedOffset": 16, "requestedLimit": 50, "totalLines": null, "wasTruncated": true}, "outputText": "", "durationMs": 0.0, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Read", "message": "50 lines from C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\src\\any3dview\\retained.py", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": "Auto-approved: allowed by your permission rules", "suffix": "(truncated)"}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "smart"}}
{"id": "reasoning-aa5c527a-97d1-5a56-8a14-049593f4b420-0", "sessionId": "fa4f1d4c-0b72-534f-d894-716a6ea09ae7", "turnId": "turn-859e7767a6871daa8f5c61af8f0fd007", "createdAt": 1792409238647, "updatedAt": 1792409238647, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Good. `visible = (depth > near) & (depth < far)` — strict. So `visible_all.all()` implies depths strictly inside near/far — matches `_surface_outline_segments` fast-path condition `depths.min() > near and depths.max() < far`. Consistent. Good — no near/far boundary counterexample there.\n\nWait — but there's a subtlety: `_project_world_points` uses `frame.scale / frame.aspect` for x, while `_surface_outline_segments` fast path uses `scale = .5*height/tan(fov/2)` and `x = .5*width + cam_x*scale/cam_z`. Are these the same? frame.scale and frame.aspect — need to check _FrameProjection. If frame.aspect = width/height and frame.scale = height/tan(fov/2)... Let's check: in _project_world_points: x = (cam_x * scale/aspect / z + 1)*.5*width. If scale = height/tan(fov*.5) and aspect = width/height, then scale/aspect = height²/(tan·width)... hmm that doesn't look right. Let me actually read _FrameProjection. Actually: x_ndc = cam_x * f / z where f = 1/(tan(fov/2)*aspect). So x = (cam_x * scale/(aspect·z) + 1)*.5*width with scale = 1/tan(fov/2)? Then scale/aspect/z = f/z. And y = (1 - cam_y*scale/z)*.5*height — with scale = 1/tan(fov/2), y_ndc = cam_y*f_y/z with f_y = 1/tan(fov/2) = scale. Consistent if frame.scale = 1/tan(fov/2) and aspect = width/height.\n\nBut `_surface_outline_segments` fast path (retained_viewer 3543-3550) computes its own: scale = .5*height/tan(fov/2); x = .5*width + cam_x*scale/z; y = .5*height - cam_y*scale/z. With scale' = .5*height/tan: x = .5*width + cam_x*(.5*height/tan)/z. Compare with _project_world_points: x = .5*width + cam_x*(scale/(aspect·z))*.5*width = .5*width + cam_x*(scale*.5*width/aspect)/z. If aspect = width/height: = .5*width + cam_x*(scale*.5*height)/z. So they match iff frame.scale == 1/tan(fov/2). And y: _project_world_points: (1 - cam_y*scale/z)*.5*height = .5*height - cam_y*(.5*height*scale)/z; retained fast path: .5*height - cam_y*(.5*height/tan)/z. Match iff scale == 1/tan. Need to verify _FrameProjection.scale. Also `_project_outline_segment` at 3520ish uses the same .5*height/tan formula. But that's the central implementation (not under review). The DPR under review is the qt.py fast branch which uses screen_all from _project_world_points — consistent with the rest of _paint_software (faces use screen_all too). So even if formulas differed slightly, faces and outlines in qt.py both use _project_world_points — internally consistent. The comparison target is _surface_outline_segments which is the fallback within the same painter — divergence between fast branch and fallback would be a bug. Let me check _FrameProjection to confirm scale/aspect.\n\nAlso check pixel_ratio property. Lines 440-470 and 650-660 in qt.py.\n\nAlso the signature concern: DirtyGenerations is a frozen dataclass — equality works. `handle.generations` property returns it. Signature includes it. set_active_elements bumps generations.active → cache invalidates. Good.\n\noutline_groups: entry[\"outline_groups\"] set from appearance `_outline_groups` at entry creation (line 2182). If user calls set_appearance with new outline_groups, generations.appearance bumps → signature invalidates → prep rebuilt with new groups. But wait — does the entry get rebuilt too? The entry appearance update path... If entry[\"outline_groups\"] is updated without generations bump... set_appearance bumps generations.appearance presumably. Let me check retained.py _replace for \"appearance\". Line 227-228 shows set_active_elements → _replace(..., \"active\"). There's likely set_appearance → _replace(\"appearance\"...). And mask_revision bumps in qt.py lines 144-173 for mask/appearance mutations on the software resources.\n\nBut there's a subtle one: `_outline_groups` invalidation mutates entry[\"outline_groups\"] = None when topology mismatch. If topology changed, generations.topology bumped → prep invalidated anyway. Fine.\n\nAnother subtle one: the drawing condition `if self.show_mesh_lines and (appearance.get(\"mesh_lines\") or entry.get(\"outline_groups\") is None)`. Hmm, `show_mesh_lines` — a viewer-level toggle. Not in signature, but it only gates whether outlines are drawn at all, not their content. Fine.\n\nNow the deeper question: cached `outline_loops` vs fallback loops when `visible_triangles` differs from `prep.triangles`. Fast branch requires visible_all.all() → triangle_visible all True → visible_triangles == prep.triangles. OK.\n\nBut wait — one more: in the fast branch, `screen_all` was computed from `world` = prep.world. prep.world includes displaced positions. `_surface_outline_segments` fallback computes depths from the same `world` passed in (prep.world). Consistent.\n\nNow potential counterexample areas remaining:\n\nA. Signature omits `mesh_lines`, `line_color`, `line_width`, `opacity`, `point_size`, `point_color` appearance keys — but those only affect styling at paint time (appearance dict read per frame), not cached geometry. Fine. But `mesh_lines` gates outline drawing — read per frame. Fine.\n\nB. Signature omits `outline_groups` content. Path: entry[\"outline_groups\"] changes without generations/mask_revision bump? Where is entry created/updated — line 2182 `\"outline_groups\": _appearance.get(\"_outline_groups\")`. Need to check when entries are rebuilt: if set_appearance triggers entry rebuild + generations.appearance bump, prep invalidates. But what if the entry is rebuilt from `_appearance.get(\"_outline_groups\")` while handle generations NOT bumped? E.g., viewer-level `set_appearance(handle, ...)`? Let me check how entries get (re)built and whether outline_groups can change without signature change. Actually there's another path: `_outline_groups` itself sets entry[\"outline_groups\"] = None on topology mismatch — but topology mismatch implies generations.topology bump (mesh replaced). Unless... mesh.triangles replaced with equal-length different topology without bump? _replace bumps.\n\nHmm, but here's a real one: cached prep.outline_loops computed with groups G. Later, entry[\"outline_groups\"] invalidated to None by `_outline_groups` (topology mismatch) — but suppose the topology mismatch arises from a chunk mesh update that doesn't bump handle.generations? Chunks: `handle.chunks` — chunk meshes updated via handle methods that bump generations? Need to check chunk update path. If chunk mesh topology changes without generations bump, prep reused with stale world AND stale loops — but that's a general cache issue, and _outline_groups would catch group mismatch only for chunk_id None (groups only for chunk_id None). For chunks, groups is always None → loops derived purely from mesh + admitted triangles. If chunk mesh changes without generations bump, prep.world stale too — but that's the central cache design; presumably chunk updates bump generations.\n\nC. The `visible_all.all()` fast branch vs fallback divergence at exactly z == near or z == far: visible is strict (`> near`, `< far`), so visible_all.all() implies strictly inside — fallback fast path condition `depths.min() > near and depths.max() < far` also strict. Consistent. But wait — the fallback in qt.py is called when NOT visible_all.all(); then _surface_outline_segments takes slow path (since some depth outside strict bounds → depths.min() <= near or max >= far... careful: visible_all.all() false means some depth <= near or >= far → depths.min() <= near or depths.max() >= far → fast path in _surface_outline_segments not taken → slow path with per-segment projection. Consistent.\n\nHmm, but one asymmetry: `_surface_outline_segments` fast path condition also requires `depths.size` — if world empty, depths.size==0, min() on empty errors? They guard with `depths.size and`. In qt fast branch, visible_all.all() on empty array is True (np.all of empty is True). So empty mesh: fast branch, loops over prep.outline_loops — empty. Fine.\n\nD. NaN/inf depths: visible = (depth > near) & (depth < far) — NaN fails both → not visible. If any NaN, visible_all.all() False → fallback. In fallback, depths.min() with NaN → NaN comparisons False → `depths.min() > frame.near` False → slow path. Consistent.\n\nE. Pixel ratio: need to check `self.pixel_ratio` vs `devicePixelRatioF`. Read qt.py 440-470 and 645-700.\n\nF. One more: in the fast branch, screen coordinates from `_project_world_points` use `frame.width/height` (framebuffer size?) while painter fills `width, height = self.viewport_size` and scales by 1/devicePixelRatioF. If frame.width/height are physical pixels and viewport_size logical... need _FrameProjection and viewport_size. The faces use the same screen_all, so internal consistency holds regardless; pixel ratio affects only widths/sizes. But the task asks about pixel ratio specifically: `pixel_ratio = self.pixel_ratio` at line 1038 vs `ratio = self.surface.devicePixelRatioF()` at 1041. If self.pixel_ratio is a stale/cached value that can differ from devicePixelRatioF (e.g., window moved between monitors of different DPI before _sync_pixel_ratio runs), line widths would be scaled wrong. Check _sync_pixel_ratio and pixel_ratio property.\n\nLet me read those ranges: qt.py 440-475, 645-700. And _FrameProjection definition in retained_viewer.py.", "summary": []}
